---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp/img/lab-1-9-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `ellipse(200, 150, 80, 80);
rect(160, 190, 80, 120);
line(200, 190, 200, 310);`;
const Figure2 = `let centerX = 200;

ellipse(centerX, 150, 80, 80);
rect(centerX - 40, 190, 80, 120);
line(centerX, 190, centerX, 310);`;
const Figure3 = `let size = 80;

ellipse(200, 150, size, size);
rect(200 - size / 2, 190, size, size * 1.5);`;
const Figure4 = `let r = 200;
let g = 60;
let b = 90;

fill(r, g, b);
ellipse(200, 150, 80, 80);

fill(r, g + 100, b);
ellipse(300, 150, 80, 80);`;
const Task1 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(220);

  fill(120, 180, 220);
  ellipse(200, 120, 90, 90);
  rect(155, 170, 90, 130);
}
`;
const Task2 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(220);

  let towerX = 200;
  let towerWidth = 90;

  fill(120, 180, 220);
  ellipse(towerX, 120, towerWidth, towerWidth);
  rect(towerX - towerWidth / 2, 170, towerWidth, 130);
}
`;
const Task3 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(220);

  let towerX = 200;
  let towerWidth = 90;

  fill(120, 180, 220);
  ellipse(towerX, 120, towerWidth, towerWidth);
  rect(towerX - towerWidth / 2, 170, towerWidth, 130);

  // Draw a second, smaller tower to the left of the first
  //   its x should be worked out from towerX, not typed in
  //   its width should be worked out from towerWidth, not typed in
  //   give it a different fill color
}
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.9: p5 Lab 2 &mdash; Variables That Draw</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />


        <!-- What You're Building -->
        <section id="target">
          <h2>What You're Building</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image src={TargetImage} alt="A dark canvas showing a tall tower built from stacked rectangles, drawn from a small set of variables" width={400} class="img-fluid rounded" />
          </div>

          <p>By the end of <b>Task 2</b> a handful of variables will control this whole tower. Change one number and every part of it moves.</p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Task 3 is not shown.</b> That one is yours to work out.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />
        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.9</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">p5 Lab 2 &mdash; Variables That Draw</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Nothing New</p>
              <p>One Variable, Several Places</p>
              <p>Computing From a Variable</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>No New Ideas Today</h2>
              <p>
                Variables from Activity 1.3. The canvas from Activity 1.5. That
                is the entire toolkit.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>No New Ideas Today</h2>
              <p>
                What is new is <i>seeing</i> what a variable is worth. In the
                terminal a variable saves you some typing. On a canvas it moves
                a whole picture at once.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>The Problem With Numbers</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure1}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Three shapes, all lined up at x = 200. To move this whole thing
                right by 50 pixels, how many numbers do you have to change?
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>The Fix</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure2}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Now it is <b class="text-sn-theme">one number</b>. Change{" "}
                <CodeSnippet
                  code="centerX"
                  lang={"javascript"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> and everything moves together, still lined up.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Look at the Rectangle</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet
                  code="rect(centerX - 40, 190, 80, 120)"
                  lang={"javascript"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                />
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A rectangle is placed by its corner, so centering it means
                shifting left by half its width. The variable does not just get{" "}
                <i>used</i> &mdash; it gets <b class="text-sn-theme">calculated
                with</b>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Sizes Too</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure3}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                One <CodeSnippet
                  code="size"
                  lang={"javascript"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> drives the circle, the rectangle's width, its height, and its
                horizontal position. Change it and the shape scales as a unit.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Colors Are Just Numbers</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure4}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                <CodeSnippet
                  code="g + 100"
                  lang={"javascript"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> makes the second circle greener than the first, and stays
                related to it. This is exactly the arithmetic from Activity 1.4.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Rule for Today</h2>
              <p>
                If two numbers in your sketch are <i>related</i>, one of them
                should be calculated from the other, not typed in separately.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Typed-in numbers drift apart the moment you change something.
                Calculated ones cannot.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Replacing repeated numbers with a variable</li>
                <li>Calculating a position or size from a variable</li>
                <li>Driving color with variables</li>
                <li>Changing one value and predicting the whole result</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Hard-coded value</dt>
                  <dd>
                    A number typed directly into the code instead of being stored
                    or calculated.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Derived value</dt>
                  <dd>
                    A value worked out from another value rather than typed in.
                  </dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>p5.js</b> sketch named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">1-9-variable-canvas</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everything goes inside <CodeSnippet
              code="setup()"
              lang={"javascript"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            /> again. Nothing moves yet.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: A Tower, With Numbers</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it. You should see a simple tower.</li>
            <li>
              Now move the whole tower 60 pixels right, by editing numbers.
              Count how many you had to change.
            </li>
            <li>Put it back at 200 before continuing.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 2 — Variables That Draw"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: The Same Tower, With Variables</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Replace the hard-coded numbers with two variables.</li>
            <li>
              The result on screen should look <b>identical</b> to Task 1. That
              is how you know you did it right.
            </li>
            <li>
              Now move the tower 60 pixels right again. This time it is one
              number.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Note</b>: the green lines with a + sign
              are new code. Don't type the + sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 2 — Variables That Draw"
            ins={{ range: "5-6, 9-10" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Plan, Then Add a Second Tower</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The comments describe a smaller tower to the left. Write the code
              under them.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Every number must be derived.</b> Its x
              comes from <CodeSnippet
                code="towerX"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />, its width from <CodeSnippet
                code="towerWidth"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />. No new literal positions.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then change <CodeSnippet
                code="towerX"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> to 260 and run. <b>Both</b> towers should move, still correctly
              spaced. If only one moved, something is still hard-coded.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 2 — Variables That Draw"
            ins={{ range: "11-15" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): One Knob</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a variable at the top called <CodeSnippet
                code="scale"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> and use it so that changing it alone resizes{" "}
              <i>both</i> towers together.
            </li>
            <li>
              Test it at 0.5 and at 1.5. Everything should stay in proportion and
              stay on the canvas.
            </li>
            <li>
              This is harder than it looks. Getting one number to control a whole
              composition is the actual skill.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your finished sketch should show all of the following.</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Two towers, the second smaller and to the left of the first.</li>
            <li>Each tower a circle sitting on a rectangle, properly centered.</li>
            <li>The two towers in different colors.</li>
            <li>
              <b>The real test:</b> changing <CodeSnippet
                code="towerX"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> to any value moves both towers together and keeps them lined up.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            If changing that one number breaks the layout, you have a hard-coded
            value left somewhere. Find it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              In Task 1 you moved the tower by editing numbers. How many did you
              change, and what would happen if you missed one?
            </li>
            <li>
              Explain why <code>towerX - towerWidth / 2</code> centers the
              rectangle, in your own words.
            </li>
            <li>
              You have now used variables in the terminal and on a canvas. Which
              made their usefulness more obvious to you, and why?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
